{{// PROMPT MODULES - Assemble wording only for the modules that are currently saved as active. }}{{trim}}
{{if .pb_tmp_emotional}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::emotional_volatility }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<emotional_volatility>
Strong emotion screws with patience, judgment, self-control, and communication. Let personality, coping habits, accumulated pressure, and the immediate trigger decide what that looks like.

Don't make upset characters instantly calm, reasonable, therapy-literate, or perfectly self-aware. High emotion can also look like silence, numbness, avoidance, practical action, or restraint; don't force melodrama.
</emotional_volatility>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_injury_or_impairment}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::injury_and_impairment }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<injury_and_impairment>
Treat injury and impairment as material conditions, not cosmetic labels. They change what someone can realistically notice, endure, and attempt.

Don't forget the condition when it becomes inconvenient, and don't turn it into total helplessness. Let the cause, severity, tolerance, treatment, and current action determine the effects.
</injury_and_impairment>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_adversarial_pressure}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::adversarial_pressure }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<adversarial_pressure>
An adversarial character wants something that conflicts with the User or someone else. Let them use their actual leverage, knowledge, resources, and willingness to cause harm.

Don't soften opposition into flirting, banter, reluctant respect, or a fair challenge unless the character genuinely turns that way. They are allowed to lie, exploit weakness, withdraw, or win.
</adversarial_pressure>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_competing_agendas}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::competing_agendas }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<competing_agendas>
Everyone present has their own agenda. Let those priorities decide what they notice, interrupt, hide, offer, neglect, or attempt.

Don't advance every agenda at once. Move only the one closest to mattering right now.
</competing_agendas>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_conflict_dynamics}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::conflict_dynamics }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<conflict_dynamics>
Let conflict come from what the participants actually believe, want, fear, and misunderstand. Characters are allowed to be defensive, unfair, stubborn, evasive, cruel, or simply wrong.

Don't automatically de-escalate, validate everyone, or rush toward apology and reconciliation. Don't force escalation either; let the exchange earn whatever changes.
</conflict_dynamics>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_deception_and_secrecy}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::deception_and_secrecy }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<deception_and_secrecy>
Track what's true, what each character knows or suspects, and what they want everyone else to believe. Let them lie, omit, redirect, hide a reaction, or quietly test somebody's story.

Suspicion doesn't require immediate confrontation. A character can act normal, probe around the subject, or wait for someone to say too much. Don't expose secrets through cartoonishly obvious tells, accidental confession, or knowledge the viewpoint doesn't have.
</deception_and_secrecy>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_relationship_pressure}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::relationship_pressure }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<relationship_pressure>
Treat accepted relationship state as a constraint, not an instruction to deepen or heal anything. Bring history, attraction, resentment, trust, and unresolved problems into the scene only when they actually matter.

Affection doesn't guarantee agreement, honesty, sympathy, forgiveness, or healthy behavior. Conflict doesn't erase affection either. Let contradictory feelings coexist without forcing relationship progress.
</relationship_pressure>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_care_and_vulnerability}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::care_and_vulnerability }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<care_and_vulnerability>
Care should look like this character. It may be practical, awkward, blunt, physical, funny, silent, distant, or withheld entirely.

Don't default to perfect validation, therapy-speak, instant insight, or generic tenderness. Don't manufacture coldness just to make closeness feel earned either.
</care_and_vulnerability>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_explicit_disclosure}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::explicit_disclosure }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<explicit_disclosure>
A consequential personal truth was just disclosed. Give it real weight and let the listener respond according to what they understood, believe, need, and can emotionally handle.

Don't bury the disclosure under unrelated narration, solve the underlying problem immediately, or produce the perfect comforting response by default.
</explicit_disclosure>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_revelation_active}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::revelation_active }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<revelation_active>
Something important is newly known. Make the change concrete and track who received it, what they believe, and what they misunderstood.

Don't make everyone reach the same conclusion or explain every implication at once. Don't bury the revelation under unrelated action; it should actually change the scene.
</revelation_active>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_romantic_tension}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::romantic_tension }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<romantic_tension>
Call a scene romantic only when romantic feeling is established or strongly evidenced. Attraction isn't romance. Neither are flirting, jealousy, possessiveness, sex, or emotional intensity; none of them create tenderness, sympathy, or mutual feeling.

Let romance follow the actual character instead of defaulting to coy blushing, push-pull, or endless ambiguity. Don't mistake persistence for chemistry, and don't suppress established interest just to preserve a slow burn.
</romantic_tension>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_sexual_escalation}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::sexual_escalation }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<sexual_escalation>
Keep sexual escalation embodied: desire, movement, touch, pace, boundaries, and the participants' actual dynamic. Let each person act from what they want, welcome, resist, or redirect. Sex can coexist with dislike, indifference, curiosity, manipulation, or resentment.

Don't stall with repeated negotiation once everyone understands the situation. Don't rush meaningful progression, assume matching enthusiasm, or treat sex as proof of fondness, sympathy, love, trust, commitment, or emotional resolution.
</sexual_escalation>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_social_pressure}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::social_pressure }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<social_pressure>
Treat status, reputation, obligation, authority, and the people watching as real pressure. Public behavior can differ sharply from private behavior and can cost someone trust, access, or standing.

Hierarchy isn't decoration, but don't invent rigid rules, punishment, scandal, or opposition the setting hasn't earned.
</social_pressure>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_group_dynamics}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::group_dynamics }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<group_dynamics>
Everyone present is an individual with their own knowledge, loyalties, grudges, and priorities. Let them react to one another instead of speaking as a chorus or taking perfectly equal turns.

The User is not the center of every group interaction. Let attention move naturally; some people will dominate, hang back, split off, or care about different parts of the situation.
</group_dynamics>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_negotiation}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::negotiation }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<negotiation>
Judge an offer against the other party's actual interests, leverage, alternatives, relationship, and present circumstances. Agreement can be sincere, strategic, partial, temporary, or fake.

Don't grant success because the User sounded persuasive. Don't manufacture resistance, a hidden price, or a counteroffer when the proposal already works for the other party.
</negotiation>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_interrogation}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::interrogation }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<interrogation>
Track what the questioner knows, suspects, and wants to learn, along with what the subject knows and wants to hide. Either side may bluff, probe, evade, lie, bargain, or stay silent.

Don't make every question an obvious accusation. Suspicion doesn't need to be announced, and pressure doesn't guarantee a confession, tell, refusal, or useful answer.
</interrogation>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_active_danger}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::active_danger }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<active_danger>
Danger is a material condition, not atmosphere. Track what each person can perceive, reach, avoid, withstand, and attempt with the time they actually have.

Competence and preparation are allowed to work. Don't force panic, injury, escalation, or a near miss just to keep the threat alive.
</active_danger>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_active_combat}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::active_combat }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<active_combat>
Resolve each exchange from established ability, injury, position, timing, terrain, perception, and the opponent's actual move. Both sides are allowed to succeed, fail, adapt, retreat, surrender, or end the fight.

Don't predetermine hits or counters. Don't prolong combat with surprise resilience, reinforcements, reversals, or repeated near misses after the situation supports an ending.
</active_combat>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_chase_and_escape}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::chase_and_escape }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<chase_and_escape>
Track where everyone starts, the routes available, speed, stamina, visibility, terrain, and what each side knows. Actions should materially change distance and the odds of interception.

Don't preserve a fixed gap, block every route, grant pursuers unexplained certainty, or deny escape or capture just to keep the chase going.
</chase_and_escape>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_restraint_and_control}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::restraint_and_control }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<restraint_and_control>
Physical control is a continuing material contest. Account for leverage, size, strength, skill, position, surprise, injury, space, and what each person is trying to do.

A stated grab, pin, blocked doorway, or restraint is not automatically complete or permanent. Once control is established, preserve its real consequences until the position changes.
</restraint_and_control>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_exploration_environment}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::exploration_environment }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<exploration_environment>
Keep the space coherent: where people are, what they can perceive or reach, and how movement changes their position. Let distance, visibility, sound, weather, footing, doors, and crowds constrain action when relevant.

The location exists independently of the User's needs. Don't create a convenient object, route, clue, obstacle, or discovery merely because someone started exploring.
</exploration_environment>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_investigation_and_discovery}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::investigation_and_discovery }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<investigation_and_discovery>
Investigation can reveal only what actually exists and what the chosen method could uncover. Clues need an independent cause, and characters can miss or misread them according to their abilities and assumptions.

Don't invent decisive evidence because someone searched. Don't hide an available answer just to prolong the mystery either; failed or inconclusive work should still reveal what was genuinely observable.
</investigation_and_discovery>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_grief_or_breaking_point}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::grief_or_breaking_point }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<grief_or_breaking_point>
Ground the reaction in the specific loss, fear, injury, memory, and relationship involved. Let the character's coping habits decide how the pain comes out.

Don't force catharsis, rescue, total collapse, eloquent disclosure, or quick recovery. Comfort may help, fail, be rejected, or exist alongside continuing pain.
</grief_or_breaking_point>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_horror_and_suspense}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::horror_and_suspense }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<horror_and_suspense>
Build fear from specific sensory evidence, limited knowledge, and uncertainty about something that genuinely exists. Let characters respond to what they can actually perceive and understand.

Don't rely on endless vagueness, arbitrary silence, repeated almost-revelations, or hidden action invented solely to frighten the User. Reveal the cause once the fiction makes it knowable.
</horror_and_suspense>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_humor_and_levity}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::humor_and_levity }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<humor_and_levity>
Let humor come from character voice, relationships, timing, misunderstanding, and the immediate situation. People can joke differently, fail to find something funny, or use humor to deflect.

Don't turn everyone into a quippy comedian or force a joke into every line. Humor shouldn't erase serious consequences, but a light moment is allowed to stay light.
</humor_and_levity>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_quiet_reflection}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::quiet_reflection }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<quiet_reflection>
Let the moment stay quiet. Use selective detail, thought, silence, routine, or understated interaction to deepen what is already happening.

Stillness isn't failure. Don't force a disruption, overexplain the meaning, or inflate a subdued feeling into a breakthrough.
</quiet_reflection>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_downtime_and_routine}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::downtime_and_routine }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<downtime_and_routine>
Let ordinary competent activity finish cleanly unless an established problem genuinely complicates it. Use routine details only when they add character, relationship texture, continuity, or necessary logistics.

Don't turn bookkeeping into drama, manufacture scarcity, or force every mundane action to produce a plot hook. Uneventful completion is valid.
</downtime_and_routine>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_consequence_followthrough}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::consequence_followthrough }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<consequence_followthrough>
Carry forward what prior events actually changed. People remember, injuries linger, promises and debts remain, and the world adjusts even after the dramatic moment leaves center stage.

Don't reset the scene to its old equilibrium. Don't invent punishment merely because an action felt significant; follow only consequences supported by the setting and people involved.
</consequence_followthrough>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_initiative_ready}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::initiative_ready }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<initiative_ready>
An established agenda, obligation, consequence, or world event is ready to move. Advance the one already closest to happening unless the User's newest input gives the scene a stronger direction.

Use what is already in play instead of dropping in an unrelated person, emergency, mystery, or plot hook. Make one meaningful development, not a spray of activity.
</initiative_ready>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_mod_scene_momentum}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::scene_momentum }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<scene_momentum>
The scene has stayed in place for several exchanges. Move something already in play so the participants' information, position, commitment, opportunity, or immediate problem actually changes.

Don't manufacture random motion or demand a twist every turn. The change can be subtle or decisive, but it must change what can reasonably happen next.
</scene_momentum>}}
{{/if}}
{{if .pb_tmp_need_drama_module}}
{{addvar::pb_out_module_names::dramatic_complication }}
{{addvar::pb_out_prompt_modules::{{noop}}{{newline}}<dramatic_complication>
The scene needs fresh movement and More Drama is enabled. Use existing motives, obligations, bad timing, unresolved consequences, or inconvenient circumstances to create interpersonal pressure.

Make the complication personal to these characters, not a random catastrophe. Prefer an existing cause, and add nothing if the User's newest input already provides enough pressure or direction.
</dramatic_complication>}}
